cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Talanai, please cite it as below."
title: "Talanai: a language for describing molecular docking experiments, and a validator that refuses the ones that could not mean what they claim to mean"
abstract: >-
  Talanai is a domain-specific language in which one docking experiment is
  written down completely: receptor, site, control, ligands, search settings and
  results. A `.tal` file is meant to be read by a biologist and executed by a
  computer, in that order. The validator applies 35 rules across parsing,
  control validity, geometry, sampling, preparation, ranking and claim scope,
  at three severities, and refuses an experiment whose control does not
  validate the protocol that produced its numbers.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Rivera
    given-names: Ken Alexander T.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-2016-8507"
    affiliation: "Mabalacat City College, Mabalacat City, Pampanga, Philippines"
keywords:
  - molecular docking
  - AutoDock Vina
  - reproducibility
  - domain-specific language
  - protocol validation
  - alpha-glucosidase
  - Ziziphus

license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/aokenji/talanai-lang"

# MIT covers the software. TalanaiHub's data and prose stay CC BY 4.0, and the
# run records under validation-run/ are research data under those terms. CC BY
# is right for data and wrong for software: it is not OSI-approved, and JOSS
# requires one. See LICENSE for the scope note.

# DOI IS STILL UNSET, deliberately.
#
# Per the 2026-08-20 publication decision the Zenodo software DOI is minted
# through the GitHub integration off a release tag, and only AFTER the language
# has driven a real run. It now has: run_replication.py produced the 2026-08-25
# convergence test. A Zenodo record is permanent, so the tag should be cut when
# the repository is in a state worth citing forever. Add `doi:` once minted.
